<TopicPage slug="subjects/entomology/foundational/insects-the-dominant-animals">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Entomology studies the planet's dominant animals: insects outnumber all other animals
    combined, run most terrestrial food webs, pollinate most flowering plants — and have done so
    for 400 million years. Their success is not luck; it is five engineering decisions, each
    visible in the anatomy.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the five inventions behind insect dominance" widget="equationStep" config={successCfg} />

  <h2>Metamorphosis is the cleverest</h2>
  <p>
    Simple metamorphosis (grasshoppers) grows a miniature adult step by step. Complete
    metamorphosis — the caterpillar dissolving inside its chrysalis into a butterfly — is the
    revolutionary version: larva and adult occupy different niches, eat different foods, and
    never compete. Beetle success (the largest order, ~400,000 species) is built almost entirely
    on this trick.
  </p>

  <h2>Anatomy is an identification kit</h2>
  <p>
    Entomologists identify insects by structures: wing venation, mouthpart type, antennae shape,
    leg adaptations. Mosquito mouthparts (a needle), butterfly (a coiled straw), beetle (jaws),
    housefly (a sponge) — one appendage, four lifestyles. The later units use this kit to tour
    the major orders.
  </p>

  <h2>Why humans need the science</h2>
  <p>
    Insects pollinate perhaps a third of our food, decompose most organic matter, and transmit
    diseases (malaria, dengue) that still kill hundreds of thousands a year. Entomology spans all
    of it — pest management, pollinator conservation, disease control and forensic timelines —
    which is why a science of "bugs" is also a science of civilisation.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Triplehorn &amp; Johnson, <em>Borrer and DeLong's Introduction to the Study of Insects</em>; Gullan &amp; Cranston, <em>The Insects: An Outline of Entomology</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
